<h1>Privacy</h1>
<p class="lede">AlarmSwap is a set of plain reference pages. It doesn't ask who you are, and it keeps as little about your visit as we could manage.</p>
<p class="last-checked">Updated <time datetime="2026-11-04">November 4, 2026</time></p>

<h2>Hosting</h2>
<p>The site is static files served by <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/what-is-github-pages" rel="nofollow noopener">GitHub Pages</a>. GitHub says it logs and stores the IP address of anyone who visits a GitHub Pages site, for security purposes. That log is GitHub's, under the <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="nofollow noopener">GitHub Privacy Statement</a>; we don't get a copy.</p>

<h2>Visit counts</h2>
<p>We count page views with Cloudflare Web Analytics, a small script loaded from Cloudflare on each page. Cloudflare's documentation says Web Analytics <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/about/" rel="nofollow noopener">does not collect or use your visitors' personal data</a>, and its product page says it <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" rel="nofollow noopener">uses no client-side state such as cookies or localStorage</a> to collect those numbers. What we see are totals: page views, the pages people read, the sites that linked here, and how fast pages loaded. We can't see who you are.</p>

<h2>Search</h2>
<p>The search box uses <a href="https://pagefind.app/" rel="nofollow noopener">Pagefind</a>. It runs in your browser and looks things up in index files served from this site, so what you type is not sent to us or to a search company.</p>

<h2>No forms, accounts or cookies of our own</h2>
<p>There is nothing to sign up for and no form to fill in. The site sets no cookies of its own.</p>

<h2>Links to other sites</h2>
<p>Citations link to alarm makers, NFPA, USFA and CPSC, which have their own privacy policies. Today the site has no affiliate or store links. If that changes, a retailer may set its own cookies after you click one of its links; the <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure page</a> explains how those links would be marked.</p>

